          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Nikki\",\"addressee\":\"Keith\",\"anchor_quote\":\"I love you\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows love to Keith. The narration expressly treats her words as an honest admission, so presenting a loving emotional state as true is the expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The occurrence is framed as a sudden emotional admission, not as Nikki undertaking or affirming an obligation to maintain the relationship. Keith’s reciprocal declaration and his later statement that their parting need not be final are separate speech acts; they do not convert Nikki’s focal avowal into a promise or relational undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you too,” he said, his voice steady and sincere.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I have to head to the club soon, but… this doesn’t have to be goodbye, Nikki.”\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Alongside the clear avowal, the declaration has moderate expressive-discharge force: it comes out suddenly and involuntarily, with the words described as spilling out before Nikki can restrain them. It nevertheless remains a comprehensible assertion of love rather than a purely reflexive cry.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"She turned to him, her heart full. “I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual force is needed: the occurrence is adequately characterized as a spontaneous, affectively discharged avowal of love, without a distinct request, command, ritual, or other core function.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have had sex and are lying together in bed, Nikki turns to Keith with her heart described as full and suddenly blurts out the focal declaration. Keith pauses, smiles, reciprocates, and they kiss again.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Nikki is established for this occurrence. Keith receives the declaration positively and reciprocates with “I love you too,” which supplies interpersonal uptake of the avowal. His later reassurance that their separation “doesn’t have to be goodbye” is a genuinely separate act and does not establish promissory force in Nikki’s earlier words.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the supplied passages as a particular published work.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports Nikki’s immediate avowal and affect, but it does not establish detailed terms of commitment or what either person understood the declaration to obligate them to do. Later supplied material involving other relationships does not alter the force of this marked occurrence.\"}"
        }
